# Independent Patent Claim

**Claim 1:**

A ground support cable bolt comprising a plurality of steel wires stranded together, wherein each of said steel wires is formed from a steel composition consisting essentially of, by weight percent:

- carbon in the range of 0.60% to 1.00%;
- silicon in the range of 0.15% to 2.20%;
- manganese in the range of 0.30% to 1.00%;
- chromium in the range of 0.10% to 1.50%;
- sulfur in an amount not exceeding 0.030%;
- phosphorus in an amount not exceeding 0.030%; and
- the balance being iron and incidental impurities;

wherein each of said steel wires exhibits a mixed microstructure comprising martensite and retained austenite, said retained austenite being present in an amount of between 3% and 25% by volume of the microstructure, and said mixed microstructure imparting to the steel wires a combination of tensile strength greater than 2000 MPa and an elongation at fracture of at least 3.5%, such that the cable bolt is adapted to absorb dynamic loading through controlled yielding while maintaining load-bearing capacity in a ground support application.

# PATENT CLAIM LANGUAGE
## Rock Bolt Cable Assembly

---

**Claim 1.** A rock bolt cable assembly for ground support in mining and geotechnical applications, comprising:

a cable body formed from a plurality of high-strength steel wires stranded together, said steel wires having a chemical composition consisting essentially of, by weight percent:

- Carbon (C): 0.65% to 0.85%;
- Silicon (Si): 0.15% to 0.35%;
- Manganese (Mn): 0.50% to 0.90%;
- Chromium (Cr): 0.20% to 0.40%;
- Sulfur (S): not more than 0.020%;
- Phosphorus (P): not more than 0.020%;
- the balance being iron and incidental impurities;

wherein the microstructure of said steel wires comprises a dual-phase martensitic structure consisting of tempered martensite in an amount of 60% to 85% by volume and untempered martensite in an amount of 15% to 40% by volume;

a first anchoring end disposed at a first terminus of said cable body, said first anchoring end configured to expand radially upon axial loading to frictionally and mechanically engage an internal wall of a borehole; and

a second end fixture disposed at a second terminus of said cable body opposite said first anchoring end, said second end fixture configured to engage a bearing plate for transferring load from said cable body to a rock surface.

---

**Claim 2.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said first anchoring end comprises a plurality of wire strands separated into a splayed configuration defining a bulb-shaped anchor, said bulb-shaped anchor having a diameter greater than a diameter of said cable body.

**Claim 3.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said second end fixture comprises a barrel-and-wedge assembly comprising a barrel member defining a tapered bore and a plurality of wedge segments disposed within said tapered bore, said wedge segments configured to grip said cable body upon application of tensile load.

**Claim 4.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said steel wires have a tensile strength of at least 1770 MPa.

**Claim 5.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said steel wires have a tensile strength ranging from 1860 MPa to 2160 MPa.

**Claim 6.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein the tempered martensite is present in an amount of 65% to 75% by volume and the untempered martensite is present in an amount of 25% to 35% by volume.

**Claim 7.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said cable body comprises seven wire strands including a central king wire surrounded by six outer wires helically wound therearound.

**Claim 8.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said steel wires further comprise a corrosion-resistant coating selected from the group consisting of zinc, zinc-aluminum alloy, and epoxy.

**Claim 9.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein the Carbon content is 0.70% to 0.80% by weight.

**Claim 10.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein the Manganese content is 0.60% to 0.80% by weight and the Chromium content is 0.25% to 0.35% by weight.

**Claim 11.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said dual-phase martensitic microstructure is obtained by a heat treatment process comprising austenitizing said steel wires at a temperature of 900°C to 950°C, quenching in a salt bath maintained at 280°C to 320°C, and holding for a duration of 15 to 45 seconds to produce partial tempering of martensite.

**Claim 12.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said cable body has an elongation at break of at least 3.5%.

**Claim 13.** A method of manufacturing the rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, comprising:

drawing steel wire rod having said chemical composition to a target diameter;

austenitizing said drawn wire at a temperature of 900°C to 950°C;

quenching said austenitized wire in a lead or salt bath maintained at 280°C to 320°C for a period sufficient to produce said tempered and untempered martensite volume fractions;

stranding a plurality of said wires to form said cable body;

forming said first anchoring end by splaying and deforming wire strands at said first terminus; and

installing said second end fixture at said second terminus of said cable body.

**Claim 14.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said first anchoring end further comprises a deformed bulb formed by radial compression of said cable body, said deformed bulb having a length of 50 mm to 150 mm.

**Claim 15.** The rock bolt cable assembly of claim 1, wherein said assembly exhibits a minimum breaking load of not less than 250 kN when tested in accordance with a pull-out test.

---

